# REMSOL — Zed AI Assistant Rules

## Project Overview

REMSOL (Rust-based Electromagnetic Multi-layer Solver) computes **effective refractive
indices** and **field profiles** of guided photonic modes in 1D multilayer (planar slab)
structures. The core solver is written in Rust for performance and exposed to Python via
PyO3 bindings, built with Maturin.

---

## Tech Stack

| Layer           | Tool / Version                                       |
|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| Core solver     | Rust, edition 2021                                   |
| Python bindings | PyO3 v0.24                                           |
| Build bridge    | Maturin >=1.7                                        |
| Python env      | uv                                                   |
| Testing         | cargo test (Rust), pytest (Python), pytest-benchmark |
| Versioning      | python-semantic-release (bumps Cargo.toml version)   |
| CI              | GitHub Actions                                       |
| Docs            | Jupyter Book                                         |
| Logging bridge  | pyo3-log (routes Rust `log` macros to Python `logging`) |

---

## Repository Layout

```
src/
  lib.rs                — PyO3 module root; registers all Python-visible types; calls pyo3_log::init()
  enums.rs              — Polarization (TE/TM), BackEnd (Transfer/Scattering), Normalization (MaxField/Power) enums
  layer.rs              — Layer struct (n: Complex<f64>, d: f64) + LayerCoefficientVector
  multilayer.rs         — MultiLayer: solver, field calculation, Python API
  transfer_matrix.rs    — Transfer Matrix Method (TMM) backend + kz_physical helper
  scattering_matrix.rs  — Scattering Matrix Method (SMM) backend
  bin/remsol.rs         — CLI binary entry point (uses SerdeLayer shim for TOML I/O)

test/
  test_slab.py          — pytest: single slab, coupled slab, asymmetric coupled slab
  test_field.py         — pytest: MaxField/Power normalization, field_complex on lossy slab
  test_pec.py           — pytest: PEC boundary condition tests
  test_qnm.py           — pytest: QNM and leaky-mode tests (use complex_neff with Outgoing BCs)

remsol.pyi              — Python type stubs (must stay in sync with lib.rs pymodule)
pyproject.toml          — Maturin + uv configuration
Cargo.toml              — Rust crate metadata and dependencies
CHANGELOG.md            — Auto-generated by python-semantic-release
docs/                   — Jupyter Book documentation + example notebooks
.github/workflows/      — CI: test.yml, publish_pypi.yml, publish_crate.yml, pages.yml
```

---

## Critical Build Rule

**After ANY change to Rust source files, the Python extension must be rebuilt before
running Python tests or using the Python API:**

```bash
uv run maturin develop --uv
uv sync --reinstall-package remsol
```

Both commands are required. `maturin develop --uv` compiles the Rust code and installs
the new wheel, but `uv` may restore a cached version of the old package on the next
`uv run`. Running `uv sync --reinstall-package remsol` forces `uv` to pick up the
freshly built extension from the local source tree.

Forgetting one or both steps is the most common source of confusion — Python will
silently use the old compiled extension.

---

## All Commands

```bash
# Rebuild Python extension (required after any .rs change)
uv run maturin develop --uv
uv sync --reinstall-package remsol

# Run Rust unit tests (no Python needed)
cargo test --lib

# Run all Python integration tests
uv run pytest test/

# Run only benchmarks
uv run pytest test/ --benchmark-only

# Run a specific test file
uv run pytest test/test_slab.py -v

# Check Rust code without building
cargo check

# Lint Rust code
cargo clippy

# Build release-mode extension (for performance measurement)
uv run maturin develop --uv --release
```

